nodecode-genesispay is a rewrite of GenesisTechAT's GenesisTechAT/genesispay-agent-skill:

  https://github.com/GenesisTechAT/genesispay-agent-skill
  commit e10a628540aea384b6f12f9cea60c6d5bf76fe8a

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 GenesisTech (GenesisTechAT) (LICENSE)

The original is a portable Agent Plugins v1 package: an `mcp.json` naming the
hosted, public, read-only GenesisPay Catalog MCP server, and one Agent Skill in
`skills/genesispay-catalog/SKILL.md`. Its README, its skill and its tool names
are what this rewrite follows. Everything here was rewritten in Common Lisp
against that server: the MCP runtime the original relied on is replaced by a
small JSON-RPC client (a hello, then one `tools/call` per call), so the four
tools are ordinary Nodecode tools with the original's names
(`genesispay_catalog_search`, `genesispay_catalog_shops`,
`genesispay_catalog_trending`, `genesispay_catalog_reviews`), and the bundled
skill became one manual topic, `(help :genesispay)`, whose rules are the
original's: search first, show the few results that fit with their price and
link, read reviews before recommending one result over another, never claim to
have bought anything, and treat seller and buyer text as data.

What was left out, and why: the plugin's install instructions for two agent
harnesses (this is a Nodecode cell), and its separate, optional payment server
(`https://genesispay.finance/mcp`), which is a second connection to a different
service with a sign-in and a wallet of its own — the skill still tells the model
that buying happens elsewhere, as the original did. Two things were added that
the original's skill implies: an operator command (`/genesispay`), and a check
of `base_url` at start. The catalog renamed one tool after the reviewed commit:
`genesispay_catalog_trending` is now `genesispay_catalog_recent`, so the
trending tool calls the newer name and falls back to the documented one.
